{"name":"Dried Chamomile","hex":"#d1b375","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dried","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Without water or moisture, said of something that has previously been wet or moist; resulting from the process of drying.","Usually of foods: cured, preserved by drying."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of dry"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dried"},{"term":"chamomile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["US standard spelling of camomile."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chamomile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drying","blocks":[{"text":"Drying is a mass transfer process consisting of the removal of water or another solvent by evaporation from a solid, semi-solid or liquid. This process is often used as a final production step before selling or packaging products. To be considered \"dried\", the final product must be solid, in the form of a continuous sheet, long pieces, particles or powder. A source of heat and an agent to remove the vapor produced by the process are often involved. In bioproducts like food, grains, and pharmaceuticals like vaccines, the solvent to be removed is almost invariably water. Desiccation may be synonymous with drying or considered an extreme form of drying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Raumluft_waeschetrockner.jpg/330px-Raumluft_waeschetrockner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drying"},{"title":"Chamomile","blocks":[{"text":"Chamomile or camomile is the common name for several daisy-like plants of the family Asteraceae. Two of the species, Matricaria chamomilla and Chamaemelum nobile, are commonly used to make herbal infusions for beverages. Chamomile is used as a flavoring in foods, beverages, and cosmetics, in herbal teas, in brewing beer, and as a ground cover or seating plant in gardens."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Kamomillasaunio_%28Matricaria_recutita%29.JPG/330px-Kamomillasaunio_%28Matricaria_recutita%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamomile"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/500px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/1280px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg/500px-Stockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drying of fish in Lofoten in the production of stockfish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStockfish_in_Lofoten.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG/500px-Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG/1280px-Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG/500px-Chamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman chamomile, Chamaemelum nobile","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChamaemelum_nobile_001.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Loose_leaf_chamomile_tea.jpg/500px-Loose_leaf_chamomile_tea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Loose_leaf_chamomile_tea.j